--TEST--
A `@see` and an `@example` past the first line of a block are checked exactly as the opening one is, so no tag rots behind a good one — `rule:tooling/doc-comment-tags-are-see-and-example`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:tooling/doc-comment-tags-are-see-and-example`: every tag of a block is
// resolved, not just the one that opens it. The first `@see` and the first
// `@example` here are good, and the two under them are the diagnostics.
/// A ledger, documented with one target that resolves and one that does not.
///
/// @see Ledger::total
/// @see Ledger::missing
/// @example examples/ledger.nvs
/// @example examples/nothing-is-here.nvs
final class Ledger {
    public static function total(): int {
        return 0;
    }
}
echo Ledger::total(), "\n";
--FILE examples/ledger.nvs--
<?nvs
// The file the first `@example` names, so the tag under it is refused for its
// own path rather than for being the second one written.
echo "a ledger\n";
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0323]: `Ledger` has no member named `missing` for `@see` to resolve
  --> %s:8:10
  |
8 | /// @see Ledger::missing
  |          ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ no such member

error[E0324]: `examples/nothing-is-here.nvs` names no file
   --> %s:10:14
   |
10 | /// @example examples/nothing-is-here.nvs
   |              ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ no such file
   = help: the path is relative to the file that wrote it, as a `require` path is

error: aborting due to 2 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
